nt  to talk to you about  was of walgreens and it's a it's a very  interesting story  back in the days um walgreens  started as a classic  brick and mortar chain of pharmacies  and really it was no different than any  other mom-and-pop pharmacy  the difference was the scale  they had more than 8 000 stores  and the metric that they boasted was  that they had  a store within five mile distance  of 75 percent of the us population  that would have been a success criteria  right because that's how you could tell  how many people walgreens could reach  and if they were able to reach those  people they would be able to sell things  to them and that's how  they're successful but with the advent  of  the digital age we're all familiar with  how  amazon has been just disrupting a lot of  different retailers right the classic  story is  how amazon started disrupting walmart  and how walmart really had to  form a unique strategy to fight back  walgreens was a little different they  were ahead of the game  um and it basically started  as somewhat of what i would call a  coincidence  right so within the walgreens if you've  ever been in one of the stores  they have a photo lab where  people back in the days would go in to  develop their photo rolls and get the  pictures out  right so with the advent of smartphones  around  2008 um around that time  people stopped using the photo roll  cameras  and smartphones were smart enough to  take pretty decent quality images at  that time  so one of the ways walgreens was  benefiting from the photo labs was that  people come in to develop their  their pictures and as a result they  would buy something else as well  right so the foot traffic was very  beneficial to them but with the advent  of smartphones then that had gone down  so they needed to think about the  customer and  what they wanted why did they go away  and figure out how they can get them  back  turned out that they were looking they  were still looking for a way to print  their digital photos  so what they did was they created a  native  application to let the end users upload  their pictures to the app  and pick them up developed at a nearby  walgreens store to do this they had to  create and expose the necessary apis to  the internal developers  right so that they could create that  application so that was that was good  um that restored some of their traffic  not all of it  right so they said all right is there  another bigger opportunity for us  to get more foot traffic into our stores  so they they looked at how exactly were  the end users  um interacting with their photos  right what additional things were they  doing it what other additional  applications were  using to enhance their pictures it  turned out that there were plethora  of other mobile and web applications  where users  would would upload their photos for  example for holiday cards  and they would decorate the card with  their photos and off and ultimately they  would want to print it out  so they said all right how can i tap  into those applications  and somehow incentivize the developers  of those applications  to have those photos printed at  in nearby at a at a nearby walgreens  right it solved their problem of getting  uh the photos printed  and it gave more foot traffic to  walgreens  so it was in their mind a win-win  situation so what they did  was now they expanded the api program  to expose the apis to uh to partners  so that they could incorporate them into  into their application and really  created an ecosystem  so this ecosystem it started with  photo apis and expanded into  a bunch of other services that they  could offer like prescription refills  to medical and healthcare applications  like purchasing their in-store  merchandise with a partner like  postmates  like integrating walgreens reward  programs  into other partner applications and all  of that  so now with um with with expanding  into an ecosystem they're able  to really extend their reach from  one or a handful of native applications  into hundreds of partner applications  that's huge  so that was that was as a whole  an example of a successful api program  right but what made walgreens api  program successful  i think the simple answer is that they  had a relentless  focus on developers and customers right  they started with their customers first  they thought about what exactly they  wanted and they gave it to them  and the method of giving it to them was  through apis but it was still connected  to their business strategy there were  two essential parts  that played a huge role and one  of them was a developer portal and  incentives and this is how  they communicated with their developers  with their partners in addition to just  exposing the apis they also  exposed an incentive program to them  they said that hey for every dollar for  example  i'm just making numbers up but for every  dollar of revenue  that you bring in to the four-to-prince  api  for example i will incentivize you 10  cents  so now there were a lot of developers  that were making net  new photo applications just to make  revenue  um out of this program so it was it was  very popular  the second one was analytics once the  apis were being consumed  the way they really gauged how the users  and the developers were accessing  consuming the apis  gave them intel on how they could  make them better so these two components  were were crucial beyond just creating  and exposing those apis  so let's tie this back into how you  should think about  your api program right um the  the there are multiple different  approaches to build an api  program but it really boils down to two  major ones  one is a tactical approach and the other  one  is a more strategic approach so let's  talk about both of them  and see what are the pros and cons  right so um let's talk about a tactical  one first  let's make it a fun exercise all right  let's say you have  a ceo or cto of your company  come to you to say that hey i've got an  idea we have  this api service that  we should really broadcast out to our  partners  it would be a mutually beneficial move  both to us and the partners  right and he gives you the task to make  that happen  how do you do it so let's say now you  are following the tactical approach the  first thing you'll do is you'll identify  the service and you'll say hey how do i  expose this out to  let's say just one external application  for now  and the solution you come to is  just open up a firewall port right and  obviously you will need some security in  front of the service  to make sure that you're allowing just  the people that should be allowed  to access it but that's the solution  all right you open up the firewall port  you add the security  policy there the next thing is what  about rogue applications right they can  really crash your system if they call  it too too frequently in that case you  just add a rate limiting functionality  and call it a day next  what if each api call needs data from  multiple backends  how would you take care of that let's  say you have some orchestration  being performed so that an api call  comes in  talks to three four any number of  different backends  gets the response back matches it up and  passes it back to to that calling  application  now do you see what  might be a problem with this strategy  what's not in focus here  well the customer is not in focus right  you are you are really thinking about  that particular service and how you  would expose it out but you're not  really thinking about  how to who is a customer and what  experience you actually want to give  them  now let's take a look at a more  strategic way  to look at your apis what you're seeing  on the screen  right now is what we call a digital  value chain  which encompasses all of the different  entities  that would eventually in one way or the  other  interact with your apis on the left you  have your users on the right you have  your backend systems  right so traditionally what um  what what people did was think about  it from more of an exposure point of  view  and that's what i illustrated in the  previous example  you have a service and you're just  thinking about how do i expose this out  i would mitigate some of the problems  around it like security and rate  limiting that that we talked about but  essentially you just want to expose it  out  but on the other hand the success  pattern that we've seen  is the opposite right think about  the users first so when  your user is sitting on the table  opening up their laptops or interacting  with their mobile application  what is the experience that you want to  give them  once you have identified that particular  experience  then you have to think about  what kinds of applications would give  them  those experiences right and those  applications would then be made  by developers so essentially you want to  make sure you're  giving absolutely awesome apis to your  developers  so that they don't need to worry about  the complexities of your systems  and they can really focus on the  experiences that they're building for  the end users  and that's really in the hands of an api  team  and a lot of you on the call might  be part of such an api team  you would really make sure that you're  focusing  on the end user and also on your  developer  first and creating those apis designing  those apis  such that they are addressing their  needs  and eventually then you would connect  those apis to your backends  right that's where all of the  interesting data  in logic of your enterprise resides so  you would connect them to the backends  to get the data execute the logic  and all of that this is how you would  strategically view your apis but  this also poses a problem right and that  problem is from a security standpoint we  talked about  all of these entities being part  of the digital value chain but the more  entities you have  the more effort you need to put in to  make sure that they're secure  for example on the left you can have  a legitimate end user  or you can have a hacker or you can have  a bot  some bad actor that really wants to  abuse  your api you can have  really any application on the internet  interacting with  your with your apis so how do you  identify that it's the right application  that you have authenticated  um how do you de-provision access  of that application to your system if  needed so all of that's important  on the developer side who are the  developers  that are consuming your apis right are  they registered  do you know them do you are you able to  track them well  and if needed are you able to drop them  from the api program  are you able to de-provision them for  apis  are they secure enough at runtime right  are they following the best practices  and standards that your  organization has laid out both from a  regulatory  and from a compliance standpoint and  finally your backend systems  is the data coming in and out of your  backend systems  coming in and out securely is it secure  is it encrypted at rest is it encrypted  in flight  and all of that and how do you also make  sure  that you're protecting your backend  systems from  a certain overload in the api call  volume so  let's let's look at how an intelligent  api management platform  would mitigate some of these threats  and make sure that your strategy  is also taking care of security  so at apogee we actually make sure that  each of these entities are thought about  cohesively  for example for the end user you would  need behavior-based security  to identify the patterns how they're  calling your your apis  and classify them as either bad actors  or legitimate users  right you'd also want signature-based  security  to protect your systems against the  well-known osp attacks  and you might also want to do some fine  grain control like  um looking at the payload complexity um  and making sure that adheres to a  particular schema  that you have defined for requests  coming in  you may also want to protect your  backend systems from some abusive users  if they're calling you apis too  frequently and have them spike or acid  so you can do all of that on the on the  end user side  also for the applications you want to  make sure that you're identifying them  you're authenticating them through  industry standard or2 protocols  and you are restricting them or  authorizing them  to access just the apis that you give  them access to  now for your developers you may want to  make sure that these are  your trusted employees or partners and  to do so you can authenticate them  through an identity provider like octa  or ping fedrid  now talking about your apis and api  teams you'd want to make sure  that your your api teams  have access to only the apis and even  the functionalities within those  apis that they should have access to  and teams like your security team are  able to set blanket policies  for all of your apis to make sure that  your system's secure you'd be able to do  all of that  with a with an api management platform  like apogee  lastly talking about your backend  systems you would want to make sure  that the connection to them is secure  through something like mutual tls and ip  white listing  and the data that flows in and out  is encrypted both on the wire and a  trust so we talked about how to  really look at your api program  strategically right thinking about the  users first  and and also thinking about security as  you are doing it  so what exactly is needed for you to be  able to do so  the first is through developer  management  right having a portal where your  developers  can come in and learn more about your  api program  maybe part of it is your documentation  other part of it is monetization  incentives  to use your apis maybe it also has an  integration to your support portal  whatever that is but any way for you  to quickly on board and engage your  partners and developers  next is analytics right so as they start  consuming your apis  you want to see how exactly they're  consuming it  right um where are the end users located  um how are they using the applications  so  you can look at all that data and make  your api program  better the next is  operations automation so as your api  program  grows in scales you shouldn't really  need to worry about the operations  yourself  right you worry about the apis you worry  about your products  and the operations and everything else  should just happen  next is api development as you're  developing these apis one thing  to note is that they would have to be  developed quickly right your  developers and their innovation  actually depends on how fast you're  rolling out features  so that would mean that ideally you  would have  pre-baked policies like we provide in in  apogee drag and drop  really quickly change the apis iterate  them version them  and go to market faster and finally  a secure inflexible api runtime  so that you can process the incoming  requests  and the responses going out  so all of this together is  really a core part of  a good api management platform these are  features and functionalities actually  that you get right now  in apogee and one thing to note is that  a very tiny portion of this  functionality  is an api gateway so tying this back  to the original idea of our talk today  thinking beyond the api gateway when you  when you really think about the users  first and make sure  you are tying your api strategy into  your business strategy then  you would need an intelligent api  management platform to drive that api  program

